WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:02.450
A student team's model of the campus

2
00:00:02.450 --> 00:00:04.970
shuttle cut cost by thirty percent.

3
00:00:05.750 --> 00:00:08.310
The transport office rejected it at once:

4
00:00:09.030 --> 00:00:11.070
it left the night students with no bus.

5
00:00:11.860 --> 00:00:13.160
The solver was right.

6
00:00:14.030 --> 00:00:15.190
The model was not.

7
00:00:16.050 --> 00:00:16.590
Why?

8
00:00:17.940 --> 00:00:20.700
Today: why formulating a model is the

9
00:00:20.700 --> 00:00:23.600
hardest skill in optimisation, how to

10
00:00:23.600 --> 00:00:26.040
teach the modelling cycle, and how to

11
00:00:26.040 --> 00:00:28.400
assess models rather than just answers.

12
00:00:29.810 --> 00:00:32.410
An optimisation model has decision

13
00:00:32.410 --> 00:00:35.470
variables, an objective and constraints.

14
00:00:36.290 --> 00:00:37.870
Choosing them is a judgement

15
00:00:37.870 --> 00:00:39.290
about the real problem.

16
00:00:39.930 --> 00:00:42.310
A missing constraint lets the solver

17
00:00:42.310 --> 00:00:44.950
exploit a loophole, just as a missing

18
00:00:44.950 --> 00:00:47.710
rule lets an agent game its reward.

19
00:00:49.030 --> 00:00:52.150
Modelling is a cycle, not a single step.

20
00:00:52.770 --> 00:00:54.290
Understand the problem with

21
00:00:54.290 --> 00:00:57.830
stakeholders, formulate, solve, then

22
00:00:57.830 --> 00:01:00.050
validate the solution against reality.

23
00:01:00.690 --> 00:01:03.170
When validation fails, as with the

24
00:01:03.170 --> 00:01:05.370
night buses, return and revise.

25
00:01:06.850 --> 00:01:09.810
The fix is one line: every route

26
00:01:09.810 --> 00:01:12.330
must have at least one bus in every

27
00:01:12.330 --> 00:01:13.690
time period with demand.

28
00:01:14.470 --> 00:01:16.470
With it, the cost saving drops

29
00:01:16.470 --> 00:01:18.650
to eighteen percent, and the

30
00:01:18.650 --> 00:01:19.930
office accepts the model.

31
00:01:21.140 --> 00:01:22.900
Textbook exercises hand

32
00:01:22.900 --> 00:01:24.060
students the variables.

33
00:01:24.910 --> 00:01:27.470
Real work starts from a vague brief.

34
00:01:28.150 --> 00:01:31.210
Give students a short messy brief and

35
00:01:31.210 --> 00:01:33.330
a stakeholder to interview, and make

36
00:01:33.330 --> 00:01:34.910
them state every assumption.

37
00:01:36.270 --> 00:01:39.030
If you grade only the optimal value,

38
00:01:39.450 --> 00:01:41.770
students learn to chase numbers.

39
00:01:42.600 --> 00:01:45.320
Grade the formulation, the stated

40
00:01:45.320 --> 00:01:48.180
assumptions, validation against the

41
00:01:48.180 --> 00:01:51.360
brief, and a sensitivity analysis.

42
00:01:52.200 --> 00:01:55.180
A short oral defence shows who

43
00:01:55.180 --> 00:01:56.620
understands the model.

44
00:01:57.840 --> 00:01:58.300
Quick check!

45
00:01:58.960 --> 00:02:01.920
The solver found the true optimum of the

46
00:02:01.920 --> 00:02:03.920
students' model, yet the plan was

47
00:02:03.920 --> 00:02:04.280
rejected.

48
00:02:05.100 --> 00:02:07.660
Which stage of the modelling cycle failed?

49
00:02:09.020 --> 00:02:10.840
Formulation: a real

50
00:02:10.840 --> 00:02:12.460
requirement was left out.

51
00:02:13.090 --> 00:02:14.610
Validation with the transport

52
00:02:14.610 --> 00:02:16.610
office caught it, and the cycle

53
00:02:16.610 --> 00:02:17.970
sent the team back to revise.

54
00:02:19.370 --> 00:02:21.470
In the app, open the modeller lab.

55
00:02:22.090 --> 00:02:24.870
Run the truck-load model, add one rule the

56
00:02:24.870 --> 00:02:28.170
model misses, run it again, and check the

57
00:02:28.170 --> 00:02:30.010
new load against what the school needs.

58
00:02:31.350 --> 00:02:31.870
Your turn.

59
00:02:32.500 --> 00:02:35.660
Two teams report the same optimal cost.

60
00:02:36.520 --> 00:02:38.920
What best shows which team

61
00:02:38.920 --> 00:02:39.980
modelled the problem well?

62
00:02:41.150 --> 00:02:42.250
Their stated assumptions,

63
00:02:42.970 --> 00:02:45.190
validation against the brief, and

64
00:02:45.190 --> 00:02:46.990
how they defend the model orally.

65
00:02:47.560 --> 00:02:49.960
Remember: modelling is a cycle,

66
00:02:50.520 --> 00:02:52.200
validation catches missing

67
00:02:52.200 --> 00:02:54.760
constraints, and assessment should

68
00:02:54.760 --> 00:02:57.100
reward the model, not just the number.

69
00:02:58.260 --> 00:03:00.580
Try it in your next lecture: read a

70
00:03:00.580 --> 00:03:02.700
four-sentence brief about the campus

71
00:03:02.700 --> 00:03:04.940
shuttle and play the transport officer.

72
00:03:05.670 --> 00:03:07.310
Teams interview you for ten

73
00:03:07.310 --> 00:03:09.310
minutes, then write variables,

74
00:03:09.710 --> 00:03:11.130
objective and constraints.

75
00:03:11.930 --> 00:03:13.150
Reveal the night-bus

76
00:03:13.150 --> 00:03:15.570
requirement only to teams who ask.
